A||N||18||Monkeyman||Funny idle question||||5||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||12-31-2000||07:34 AM||||I was starting to install my new stereo last night and blew the fuse for the dash lights (probably a coincidence but thought I'd throw it in in case it had any significance).  Right around the same time, the idle started acting funny.  I know that the idle "searches" slightly, but all of a sudden it would search about 600 rpms worth instead of the normal 100.  Also, it was idling higher than normal (about 1650?).  Every once in a while it would idle back down to about 800 rpms then return to the odd idle.  The car was idling for a while (had to keep warm) if that makes a difference.  Drivability doesn't seem to be effected.  Should I quit worrying?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Wolfhound||12-31-2000||09:18 AM||||Monkey, May have just carboned up the oxygen sensor, while idling for a prolonged period.<P> More than likly, returning to normal driving will correct it. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||12-31-2000||09:46 AM||||If you unplugged the battery, the ECM will be running off settings from the factory until you drive it. That could account for it. The engine has more carbon and junk now than it did from the factory. My Formula runs crappy for the first few miles after the ECM is reset. The idle hunts too until the computer figures everything out.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||12-31-2000||11:48 PM||||Maybe something is just getting carboned up.  I forgot about having to drive for a while after a battery disconnect but I took the GT for a 15 mile (each way) trip today then came home and let it idle.  It did the same thing.  Almost seemed like it might have something to do with the temp (fan?).  I couldn't hear the fan come on, but I noticed that it did it when the temp reached about 190*.  Also, the fuel pump seems awfully loud.  Could that have anything to do with it?  (Does a loud fuel pump mean that it's about to go???)||||reg||1
Z||000004||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||01-01-2001||12:28 AM||||My pump was pretty loud and then silent just before it failed.  It's sitting at a Shell station about 25 miles from my home waiting for a new pump right now. <BR>My idle was not as lumpy as yours, however.  The car just lost power on the freeway, sort of recovered, and then lost power again as I got off the freeway.  It sort of ran for a few hundred more feet and then died.  It started one more time after I pushed it  into the gas station lot, but it did not run for more than a few seconds.<BR>I had been experiencing some hesitation between 2000 and 2500 rpms over the last week or so.  <BR>Maybe hearing my story will help you to decide to test fuel pressure. If I had, then I would be fixing the fuel tank at home instead of paying for someone else to do it.  The tank was full, so maybe it is better to pay this time!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||4
Z||000005||Monkeyman||01-01-2001||12:32 AM||||If the fuel pump decides to go, I'll farm out the work.  I don't do mechanical stuff.  I hope it's not about to die, but...||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||01-01-2001||09:13 AM||||The pump in that Fiero has been loud ever since the first time I saw the car. I figured it was just an aftermarket since some are known to make more noise. I bet William knows if it's been replaced or not.||||reg||1
Z||000007||william_thorniii||01-02-2001||08:13 AM||||MM,<P>It wasn't replaced during the year that I owned it.  I do not know what either of the previous owners did though.  I would not worry about the fuel pump though.  They rarely go out and almost never would contribute to a wandering idle.  It is odd that this happened because when I owned her, she had one of the steadiest V6 idles around.  My first suggestion would be to purchase a can of decarbonizer from the local parts store and spray that down the throttle body.  Make sure that you get one for fuel injected cars.  If this problem is not rectified by Spring, I will gladly see if I can figure it out.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||01-02-2001||07:09 PM||||WTIII---I'll do just that tomorrow.  If I'm still having problems in the spring, you're more than welcome to make a trip up here and help me figure it out.  <P>I probably won't be replacing the fuel pump until it goes altogether.  I called for an estimate today and the cost of replacement (plus a new fuel filter--might as well for a few extra bucks) is around $275.  A little too expensive for me.  I don't care if it's noisy as long as it isn't a sign of trouble.||||reg||1
Z||000009||fiero56||01-02-2001||08:07 PM||||I would check any electrical connections that were touched. Particularly the battery connections and such. Varying voltages from bad connections can set it off like that. Does it only idle funny meaning if you depress the gas pedal a little and have it idle at 2,000, does it still wander? Can you feel the engine "surging" under steady throttle as you drive? Try resetting the ECM by disconnecting the battery for a second. If that doesn't work, put the ECM in diagnostic mode and start it, see if the idle wanders then.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Monkeyman||01-03-2001||04:27 AM||||Drew--This seems to be an idle only problem.  It runs great when it's being driven.  It only seems to idle funny when it's been idling for a while.  (Hey--It's cold and I want to be warm while working on the stereo, etc.)  It idles fine if I give it a little gas.  Just has problems (not always) when it tries to idle on it's own.  I know that the IAC was recently replaced so that's probably not the problem.  I've checked all of the electrical connections and reset the ECM.  (BTW--The dash lights fuse blowing must have been a coincidence--no problems since although I'm using a slightly larger fuse.  10A instead of 5A--it was all I had handy.)<P>I'll try some of that decarbonizer today.  Where to I put it?  You know how unmechanical I am.  Do I disconnect the breather tube (don't know what it's called) and spray it in there or is this somethink I add to a tank of  gas?||||reg||1
Z||000011||william_thorniii||01-03-2001||02:49 PM||||MM,<P>Yes, you remove the intake tube from the  plenum.  Then spray the decarbonizer with one hand while running the throttle up to around 3k with the other.  Try not to stall out the engine and also try to avoid revving it too high.  56 has recently used some of this stuff.  Perhaps he will have more suggestions.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000012||ROB MAC||01-03-2001||04:06 PM||||You may have shorted the isolation relay in your lighting system which will cause a screwy idle.||||reg||1
Z||000013||ThatRickGuy||01-03-2001||04:21 PM||||MM - on that fuse, I spent a full night re-wiring my sterio trying to find which line was causing the short. it turned out to be the ground line the old sterio was run off. re-run a higher guage wire up to the bolts on the steering colomn and the shorting problems should go away.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Monkeyman||01-05-2001||09:27 PM||||I haven't had any more shorting problems.  I started to get a terrible hesitation while driving so I added a bottle of STP gas treatment the other day.  So far it seems to be running better.  It hasn't been as cold so I haven't been idling it while I work.  I don't know if the idling problem was caused by carbonation or if the gas treatment helped??  I'll see what happens next week when the weather turns much colder again.  I have the stereo wired up to the original wiring.  All that's wired up is the power (both constant and switched) and the ground.  The speaker connections will run through the amp (if I ever get the rack built).  Right now I just have a very expensive clock in the dash. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000015||fiero56||01-06-2001||12:31 AM||||You might want to try running some "drygas" or similar through it, if the STP doesn't mention removing water from the fuel system too. Can't hurt, and should only be a buck. <P>I only drove that car a couple times, but it always seemed to be in perfect condition. And as William stated, it had a rock solid idle. If you want to try swapping sensors Dave, I have a handful of know good ones I could mail to you to try out.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Monkeyman||01-07-2001||07:52 PM||||I have no doubt that this is a new problem.  It's entirely possible that things are just popping up now that it's become a daily driver.  Suggest some sensors to replace and I'd love to borrow some of yours.  It seems to be better now that I've used a bottle of the STP stuff, but I'll use another bottle with my next fillup (tomorrow).  It's supposed to get colder this week.  We'll see if that makes any difference.||||reg||1
Z||000017||theogre||01-08-2001||12:13 AM||||MM get the right fuse in there. That fuse lives between the dimmer transisitor and the lights. It protects the transistor. With a 10 in there you could blow the transistor before the fuse next time. That thing is allot harder to get to than the fuse.||||reg||1
Z||000018||fiero56||01-08-2001||12:04 PM||||Monkeyman,<P>I can ship out two Idle Air Control valves(the ECM uses them to control the amount of air allowed in at idle), a Throttle Position Sensor(this tells the ECM how far open the throttle is), and some MAP(Manifold Pressure) sensors(they control timing under load among other things).<P>All of these sensors are accesible from the top of the engine, and only the IAC is a pain in the butt to change.<P>Let me know.||||reg||1
